rk of 2:56.16 was altitude aided, but would last until it was equaled at the 1988 Olympics by another US relay team and it would not be broken until the 1992 Barcelona Olympics. Despite the acclaim given to " [:a {:href "/athletes/78091"} "Bob Beamon"] "’s long jump mark, this was the world record from Ciudad de México that lasted for the longest period of time. The US actually did not lead on the opening leg, as " [:a {:href "/athletes/72734"} "Charles Asati"] " put Kenya four metres ahead of " [:a {:href "/athletes/78745"} "Vince Matthews"] ". But the outcome was settled on the second leg when 400 bronze medalist " [:a {:href "/athletes/78418"} "Ron Freeman"] " ran 43.2, the fastest leg to that time, and still a remarkable time. With " [:a {:href "/athletes/78595"} "Larry James"] ", the 400 silver medalist, and " [:a {:href "/athletes/78380"} "Lee Evans"] ", the gold medalist, running the last two legs, the race was over."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitors"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "–"] [:td {} "United States"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "3:00.7 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "2:56.1 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "label label-default"} [:abbrev {:title "World Record"} "WR"]]] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {:colspan "10"} [:a {:href "/athletes/78745"} "Vince Matthews"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/78418"} "Ron Freeman"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/78595"} "Larry James"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/78380"} "Lee Evans"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/79064"} "Tommie Smith"] [:small {} " (DNS)"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/79151"} "Geoff Vanderstock"] [:small